  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-31571",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the line. We know that $m=-2,$ so we can write the equation of the line as $y=-2x+b.$\n\nSince the line passes through the point $(0,6)$, then the $y$-intercept of the line is $b=6.$ \n\nSo, the equation of the line is $y=-2x+6.$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+6 \\\\ 0 & =-2x+6 \\\\ -6 & =-2x \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=3.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(3,0).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
